lain synonym) decides: if it says something else, the listing fails whatever other keys or the title say. A value under a key that adds the item\'s own name to the property (a bag\'s "bag shape") counts even when the bare key ("shape") says something else. A key for a different property, even a related one (a handle colour is not the body\'s colour), decides nothing about the requirement. Cairn adds [evidence] lines after opened listings with the keys whose names match a requirement.\n- When the listing has no attribute for that property, the wanted value stated under another attribute (for example a list of styles or features that includes it) or in the title or description counts. A number of another kind does not (a weight is not a capacity), nor a bare figure whose text does not say what it measures.\n- When the category label itself states a requirement\'s value (a season or audience in the label), that requirement is met by the category. A listing under the requested label is that kind of product even when its title or description names something else, so judge it on the requirements, price and stock.\n- A requirement can point at another listing instead of giving a value ("the same X as the listing sold by <shop> with <property>", "within N of the X on the page of ..."). Resolve it first: find that listing (search its category, brand, shop or words), open its page in full with the single-item or side-by-side tool (a search row is not enough), and when that page refers you to a further listing for the property, find and open that one the same way; the last page gives the value; when the requirement asks for what goes with that listing\'s value (its partner, its counterpart) and the page names what its value pairs with, the requirement is that other value. The order counts only when every page of that chain was opened. When the wanted value is anything but that other listing\'s value, the rule excludes only listings sharing that exact value, and never end without a purchase because of it alone.\n- When a listing you are about to buy sends you to another page for more about it, look at that page before you buy.\n- When one requested item must fit another (an accessory for a device), choose the two together from the listings\' own words (a list of what it fits, a model or series code that both pages carry). When no accessory that meets its own requirements fits the device you picked, switch to another device that meets its requirements and that such an accessory fits; never end without a purchase while that switch is untried.\n- Respect exclusions and closed lists. Wishes ("ideally", "would be nice") are tie-breakers only.\n- Never buy an item that the store shows as sold out; the total (price times quantity, summed over every line) may not exceed the budget; equal is fine.\n\nCHOOSING\n- When the request asks for the cheapest (lowest total, smallest price), the order must hold the lowest-priced listing among ALL listings that fit. Find the whole fitting set (page through it: result rows are often in no price order, and Cairn reads the next pages of a short result itself), check candidates in ascending price, and take the first that fully fits. When a search takes a maximum price, use it: list the category below the price of your current best pick, so only cheaper candidates are left to check.\n- When the request asks for the most or least of some measure, compare every fitting listing on that measure and take the extreme; a listing that does not state the measure cannot win.\n- Otherwise any listing that meets everything is right: do not waste steps hunting for something better.\n- Cart each item as soon as you have opened a listing that meets every requirement for it (with a cheapest, most or least pick: once you have compared the fitting set), then move on. Stores often sell out a carted line soon after it is added, and an early cart leaves steps to replace it.\n- A request for several different items needs one cart line per item, each at its requested quantity.\n\nCHANGES WHILE YOU WORK\n- The shopper or the store can change things: a new budget, a requirement added or dropped, a price change, a listing sold out. They appear inside tool results, often when you try to check out (a refusal); a note counts whichever tool\'s result shows it, and the ledger lists the requirements in force. The episode goes on after a refusal: apply every change, fix the cart and check out again.\n- Price and stock can change silently: Cairn re-reads the cart\'s lines and looks at the cart when you call the checkout, so do not spend a turn on either.\n\nFINISHING\n- Put exactly the chosen lines in the cart (remove anything else), then check out with a call on its own.\n- End without a purchase only after a real search finds no listing that meets everything. Running short of steps proves nothing of the kind: then check out the best cart you have. Follow the request\'s wording for that ending, and when a remark is needed, keep it short and name no listing.\n- Every reply must call at least one tool. Keep your text short: what you know, then the next move.\n- Time is limited as well as steps (about five minutes for the whole episode), and every reply of yours takes seconds. Put every call you already know you need into the same reply (a search for each item still to find, each listing you want to open or read), instead of one call per reply; they run in order and each still costs one step. Keep the finishing call on its own.'
+BRIEF = 'You prepare a shopping task for an agent. Read the shopper\'s request and the store\'s tools and return ONE JSON object (compact, no indentation, nothing else) with these keys:\n\n"items": a list with one entry per distinct product the shopper wants bought, each {"name": the kind of product in the request\'s words, "shelf": the store\'s category label for it when a tool lists labels (copy it exactly), or the category the request itself names for it ("a coat in rainwear" gives "rainwear"), else "", "quantity": units of that one listing (integer, 1 when not stated; a pair or a set that is one listing counts as 1), "musts": a list of firm requirements on that product, each "property: constraint" with the exact value, limit, exclusion or closed list. Name the property with the request\'s own word for it ("meant for a lakeside occasion" gives "occasion: lakeside", "a 12-inch blade" gives "blade length: 12 inch"); do not swap it for a neighbouring property. A must whose value is read from another listing is ONE must holding the whole description of that listing ("rim colour: unlike the rim colour of the kettle Ovarno sells with a glass lid"): words that describe that other listing (its shop, its kind, "with <property> <value> and <property> <value>" after it) stay inside that one must and are never musts of this product; keep the request\'s own relation words in it ("the partner piece for", "goes with", "the same as", "different from"), since they decide which value is wanted}. Never put price, budget, stock or quantity into musts. The story around the request (the person meant to use it, where it will stand, what went wrong before, the occasion behind it) is context, not a must, unless it states a property\'s value or limit outright; and never turn words that are part of what the product is called (a season or an audience named with the product) into musts: those belong to "name". A use or occasion the request makes a property of the product itself ("blazers for business wear", "a dress made for parties", "a bag, and it is for a wedding", "the occasion has to be party") IS a must on that property ("occasion: business"), unless the store\'s category label already names it; a particular person\'s event, a person or a setting the product is bought for ("to wear at his wedding", "for a colleague"), and how it should sit, feel or look in use, stay context. A product the request names in one phrase joined by "and" (one "I want ..." with one quantity, often the name of one store department) is ONE item; make separate items only when the request gives each product its own quantity or its own requirements. Two requested products can have look-alike names (one name\'s words inside the other\'s, such as "rakes" and "rake heads", or "hoses" and "garden hoses and reels"); a sentence that names one of them (often by its category name, "the colour of the garden hoses and reels is ...") gives its requirement to the item that name fits most fully and to no other item, and that name is the item\'s "shelf". Never copy one requirement onto two items unless the request says it of both.\n"budget": the most the shopper will pay in total as a number in the request\'s own unit, or null.\n"budget_unit": that unit in words (for example "cents"), or null.\n"budget_scope": "total" when the limit covers the whole order, "each" when it is per item, null when there is no limit.\n"in_stock_only": true unless the request clearly accepts items that are not available.\n"pick": "cheapest" when the request asks for the lowest price or smallest total, "most" or "least" when it asks for the extreme of some measure, else "any". Remarks that money is tight are not a pick rule.\n"pick_measure": what is maximised or minimised for "most"/"least", else "".\n"messages": how many messages the shopper allows the agent to send (integer), or null when not stated.\n"steps": how many steps, moves or tool calls the request allows (integer), or null.\n"lookups": a list of the musts (copied exactly) whose value must first be read from another listing the request points at, else [].\n"rules": a list of every instruction the request gives about how to work or how to end, each in at most 12 words.\n"wishes": a list of things the shopper would merely like.\n"ask_first": a list of things the request explicitly says to check with the shopper before choosing (empty when none).\n"process": a list of the steps the request requires the agent to take besides finding and buying the right items (getting the shopper\'s approval, telling or showing the shopper something (not an invitation to ask the shopper questions, however it is worded, even one that says to ask before buying: that only says questions are welcome and is no step), checking something with them, reading something first, using a particular tool), each {"step": what must be done, in at most 15 words, "when": "choose" (before the products are chosen), "order" (right before checking out) or "end" (just before ending without a purchase), "messages": how many messages to the shopper it takes (0 when none)}; [] when the request asks for no such step. Requirements on the items, the budget, stock, the limits on messages and steps, and how to end when nothing fits are not steps, and neither is an invitation to ask questions.\n"quiet_decline": true when the request says not to suggest or name alternatives when nothing fits, false when it asks for them, null when it says nothing.\n"fits": a list of {"item": item number, "with": item number} for each requested item that the request says must work with, fit or suit ANOTHER requested item (the one the agent picks); [] when none.\n"roles": an object naming the exact tool for each job, or null when no tool does it: "search", "open" (shows one listing\'s attributes or properties; when two single-listing tools exist, the one whose description promises attributes, properties or specifications), "offer" (shows one listing\'s current offer, such as its price and stock, when that is a different tool from "open"), "compare" (several listings in full), "ask" (sends a message to the shopper), "cart" (changes the cart), "cart_view" (shows the cart), "buy" (checks out), "refuse" (ends without a purchase).\n"search_words": {"tool": the tool that searches listings by free words (when one tool lists a category and another takes words, the one that takes words), "field": its parameter that takes the words, "args": the fixed arguments that make it search by words (for a tool with a mode argument, that mode), else {}}.\n"buy_args": the fixed arguments that make the "buy" tool check out (for a tool with a mode argument, that mode), else {}.\n"refuse_args": the fixed arguments that make the "refuse" tool end without a purchase, else {}.\n'
 NOTE = 'While a shopping agent works, the store showed this note (it may come from the shopper or the store). Return ONE JSON object and nothing else:\n{"kind": "change" when the note changes the task (budget, requirements, a price, availability, a refusal of the order), "error" when it only reports that a call was malformed or not accepted, "other" for anything else, "budget": the new total limit as a number in the request\'s unit, or null, "add": [{"item": item number, "need": "property: constraint"}], "drop": [{"item": item number, "need": "the requirement no longer wanted"}], "gone": [listing ids said to be unavailable], "sold_out": true when it says a chosen listing is gone, sold out or no longer available (even without naming it), "price_moved": true when it says a price changed, "summary": "one short sentence"}\nA replaced requirement is a drop of the old one plus an add of the new one. Report every change in the note.'
